course: dictionary
lesson: 11441
title: Adequate means enough
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Adequate is a formal word. It means enough, or good enough for what is needed. «The tent had adequate space for four children to sleep.» There was enough space, so the tent was big enough for the job.
explain: Look for the need in the sentence. Adequate often goes with a need: adequate water, adequate light, adequate protection. Ask yourself: enough for what? Then put your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word and check it still makes sense.
explain: Be careful, because adequate can also mean only just good enough, not very good. «Her science report was adequate, but it could have been better.» The clue is the words but it could have been better, so adequate is not praise.
fact: Adequate means enough, or good enough for a need or purpose.
fact: «Adequate light» means enough light to see by, so you do not strain your eyes.
fact: «Adequate protection» means enough protection to keep you safe.
fact: Adequate can mean only just good enough, not very good.
fact: In a school report, «adequate» work is acceptable, but it could be better.
exercise: «The shelter gave adequate protection from the rain.» What does adequate mean here? | Too big to move | Made of wood and glass | Very beautiful and bright | *Enough to keep the rain off
exercise: «Our teacher said my essay was adequate, but I could do better.» What does adequate suggest? | *Good enough, but not very good | Excellent and perfect | Late and wrong | Funny and silly
exercise: «The lamp gave adequate light to read by.» Copy the word that means enough. | lamp | gave | *adequate | read
test: «There was adequate food for everyone at the picnic.» What does adequate mean? | *Enough for everyone | Too much to eat, so some was thrown away | Eaten by the dogs | Cold and rotten
test: A teacher writes «adequate» on a maths answer. What might it mean? | The answer is too long to read | *The answer is acceptable but could be better | The answer is perfect and full marks | The answer is wrong and must be thrown away
exam: Which sentence shows the work was only just good enough? | «Her drawing was stunning.» | «Her drawing was funny.» | *«His drawing was adequate.» | «Her drawing was lost.»
